                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric storage tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,000–$2,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.90–0.95</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10–15 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No gas, simple swaps</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tankless (gas)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,500–$5,000+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.81–0.97</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~20 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gas homes, endless showers, small spaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heat pump / hybrid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,500–$5,000 before rebates</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.3–4.0+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10–15 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electric homes with room and mild space</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solar with backup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest of the five</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies by climate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20+ years (collectors)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sunny climates, long ownership</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Indirect / combi</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies; competitive with existing boiler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tied to boiler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 15–20+ years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Homes with hydronic boilers</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two things stand out. First, the cheapest heater to buy is rarely the cheapest to own. An electric tank and a heat pump water heater use the same fuel, but the heat pump typically uses a fraction of the electricity, and the gap compounds every month. Second, the efficiency column is not comparable across fuels without prices. A gas tank at 0.62 can cost less to run than an electric tank at 0.93 when gas is cheap relative to power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lifespan matters more than it looks. A tankless heater that lasts twenty years replaces roughly two conventional tanks, which narrows the upfront gap considerably. Heat pump models are newer and their long-term track record is shorter, so treat their lifespan as similar to an electric tank until more data accumulates.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges Worth Remembering</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8–12 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Gas tank lifespan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~20 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tankless lifespan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3.3–4.0+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Heat pump UEF</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common setpoint</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Brand matters less than type, and less than installation quality. If you want to know who actually makes what, and which names are rebadges of the same factory, our rundown of <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands</a> covers the major manufacturers and where each sells.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Choose: A Five-Question Decision Path
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with fuel. If the house has natural gas and the gas line has spare capacity, your realistic shortlist is a gas tank, a condensing gas tank or a gas tankless unit. If the house is all electric, the shortlist is an electric tank, a heat pump water heater, or electric tankless if the panel is large and groundwater is not too cold. Changing fuels is possible but usually adds thousands to the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, look at the space. A heat pump water heater needs a large, reasonably warm room. Tankless needs a wall, a vent route and, for condensing models, a drain. A conventional tank fits where the old tank was. If the heater lives in a cramped closet inside living space, a heat pump becomes harder, and a tankless unit or a like-for-like tank becomes easier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then match the usage pattern. Large households that run several showers in a row favor tankless or a generously sized tank. Households that use most of their hot water at one fixture far from the heater may be better served by adding a small <a href="/point-of-use-water-heater/">point-of-use water heater</a> near that fixture rather than upsizing the main unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, check incentives and electrical service. Utility rebates can swing the math toward heat pump models, and panel capacity can rule out electric tankless entirely. Finally, decide how long you will stay. If you plan to move within five years, a straightforward tank replacement usually wins. If you are staying fifteen years, the higher-efficiency options have time to pay back.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK PICKS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Gas home, big family, small space: tankless. Electric home with a basement: heat pump. Tight budget or short stay: conventional tank.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Those three rules cover most houses. Solar and boiler-fed systems earn a place only when the roof or the existing boiler already points that way.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing like for like without thinking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When a tank fails, the path of least resistance is an identical tank. That is fine if you have compared the options, but many owners never do, and lock in the highest running cost for another decade. Even a rushed replacement leaves time for one phone call about a heat pump or tankless quote.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing UEF across different fuels</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A higher UEF means less energy per gallon, but energy is priced differently. A 0.93 electric tank is not automatically cheaper to run than a 0.65 gas tank. Multiply each by your local gas and electricity rates, or use the annual operating cost on the EnergyGuide label, before deciding which is cheaper to own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the panel and the gas line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Electric tankless units can demand more amps than an older panel can spare, and gas tankless heaters often need a larger gas line than a tank did. Discovering either problem after the purchase turns a weekend install into a costly upgrade. Have the electrical service and gas supply checked first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a heat pump unit in a closet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heat pump water heater pulls heat from the air around it. Squeeze it into a small sealed closet and it cools the room, loses efficiency and leans on its resistance elements. Follow the manufacturer's minimum air volume, or use a louvered door or ducting kit where the model allows.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting long-term maintenance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every type needs some care. Tanks need anode checks and sediment flushing, tankless heaters need periodic descaling in hard water, heat pumps need filter cleaning, and solar loops need glycol changes. Skipping it shortens life on all of them, so factor the routine into the decision rather than discovering it later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the main types of water heaters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There are five: conventional storage tanks (gas or electric), tankless or on-demand heaters, heat pump or hybrid heaters, solar water heaters, and indirect or combi systems heated by a boiler. Most homes choose among the first three, because solar and boiler-fed systems depend on a suitable roof or an existing hydronic boiler.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which type of water heater is most energy efficient?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Heat pump water heaters are the most efficient common type, typically rated around UEF 3.3 to 4.0 or higher, because they move heat from the air rather than generating it. Solar systems can offset even more energy in sunny climates, but they cost more and still rely on backup heating in winter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of water heater lasts the longest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tankless heaters typically last around twenty years, and well-maintained indirect tanks and solar collectors can last similarly long. Conventional gas tanks commonly last 8 to 12 years and electric tanks 10 to 15, with lifespan driven mostly by the anode rod, water hardness and how often sediment is flushed out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a conventional water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A conventional water heater is a storage tank, usually 40 to 80 gallons, that keeps water hot around the clock using a gas burner or electric elements. It is the most common type in American homes, the cheapest to buy and install, and the one most plumbers can replace the same day it fails.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is tankless better than a tank water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the house. Tankless suits gas homes that want endless showers, wall-mounted compactness and a long lifespan, and can justify a higher install cost. A tank suits tighter budgets, simple swaps and households that need high flow from several fixtures at once without upgrading gas lines or electrical service.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I switch from one type of water heater to another?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but switching adds work. Going from a gas tank to tankless often needs a bigger gas line and new venting. Going from electric resistance to a heat pump model needs space and a drain. Switching fuels entirely usually means new circuits or a new gas line, which can add thousands to the job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size water heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tanks are sized by first-hour rating, roughly 40 to 50 gallons for two to four people and larger for bigger households. Tankless units are sized by gallons per minute at your winter temperature rise. Heat pump tanks are often sized one step larger than the tank they replace because they recover more slowly.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

